**A Google Pixel can't show you who tried to unlock it.** Android counts wrong PIN, pattern and password guesses only to make the person wait before trying again. There's no list of attempts, no time, and no photo. To find out who it was, install an app that's notified of failed unlocks, like [CrookCatcher](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.harteg.crookcatcher&referrer=utm_source%3Dwebsite%26utm_medium%3Dguide%26utm_campaign%3Dpixel-who-tried-to-unlock). It takes a photo with the front camera after a wrong PIN and emails it to you with the location.

## What your Pixel does after a wrong PIN

Pixels get new Android versions first. On Android 16 QPR2 and later, Google's published waits look like this:

| Wrong guesses | Wait before the next try |
| --- | --- |
| 1 to 4 | None |
| 5 | 1 minute |
| 6 | 5 minutes |
| 8 | 30 minutes |
| 10 | 4 hours |
| 12 | 36 hours |
| 15 | 41 days |
| 20 | No more guesses allowed |

Google says Android 17 is stricter still. A few more things to know:

- **Repeating the same wrong PIN doesn't count again**, so a child tapping the same code won't use up your attempts.
- **On Android 17, the lock screen shows a link** (g.co/android/unlock) with recovery options you can open on another device.
- **If you can't unlock your Pixel at all, it has to be erased.** You can restore your data from your Google Account backup afterwards, if you know the PIN or pattern you used when the backup was made.

A "Try again in…" message is the only sign you'll see that someone tried. It doesn't say who, when, or how many times.

## Pixel settings worth turning on

These don't show you who tried, but they make your Pixel much harder to get into or take away.

### Theft protection

**Go to:**
`Settings > Security & privacy > Device unlock > Theft protection`

It's also under `Settings > Google > All services > Theft protection`.

- **Theft Detection Lock:** locks the screen if your Pixel detects it's been snatched.
- **Offline Device Lock:** locks the screen soon after the phone goes offline, so a thief can't stop it being found.
- **Failed Authentication Lock:** locks the screen when unlock attempts fail repeatedly. It's on by default; on Android 16 and later it has its own switch.
- **Identity Check:** away from trusted places, sensitive actions like changing your screen lock or turning off Find Hub need your fingerprint or face.

### Remote Lock and Find Hub

- **Remote Lock** lets you lock your Pixel from any browser at [android.com/lock](https://www.android.com/lock) with just your phone number. Turn it on under **Theft protection > Remote Lock**. You can add a security question so nobody else can use it.
- **Find Hub** shows where your Pixel is and lets you ring, lock or erase it. Make sure it's on before you need it.

For more settings, see the [top 5 Android settings to secure your phone against theft](/blog/top_five_settings).

## What your Pixel can't tell you

- **Who tried.** No photo of the person.
- **When it happened**, or how many times.
- **Where the phone was** at the time.
- **An alert while it happens.**

## How to get a photo of whoever tries to unlock your Pixel

CrookCatcher fills exactly that gap. Android notifies apps with the **Device admin** permission each time an unlock fails. CrookCatcher uses that to take a photo with the front camera, record the location and an approximate address, and email both to you. Every attempt is also saved in the app with its time.

